# Copy to .env and fill in. Never commit a filled-in copy.
#
# There is no secret in this repository. Object-storage credentials are the AWS
# SDK's own chain (environment, profile, IMDS, container role) and are never
# read from a file this service owns — a credential in a repo file is a
# credential in every clone, every CI log, and every layer of a built image.

# --- required -------------------------------------------------------------

# --- the shared cluster ---------------------------------------------------
#
# darkroom JOINS kit's shared postgres rather than running its own. Its database
# and its NOSUPERUSER role come from ONE key, and that key is named in this
# repository's own `docker-compose.yml` as `${KIT_POSTGRES_DATABASES:-darkroom}`
# — so a single-service checkout needs nothing here and this section can stay
# empty, which is deliberate rather than an oversight.
#
# DO NOT write `KIT_POSTGRES_DATABASES=darkroom` below. Compose gives `.env`
# PRECEDENCE over the `${…:-default}` written in a service's committed compose
# file, so a value here is not a default — it OVERRIDES the committed
# declaration, and the declaration stops being the reviewed, diffable statement
# of what darkroom is. The whole list belongs in ONE place, and this repository's
# compose file is it. Extend it there (comma-separated, alongside the names
# already there) when a second service shares this cluster.
#
# The two keys below are the OPERATOR's, and they are the two that are genuinely
# cluster-wide rather than darkroom's own. kit's `.env.example` documents them
# and ships both with these same defaults, so a value here agrees with kit rather
# than fighting it. A developer who wants postgres somewhere else on their host
# changes `KIT_POSTGRES_PORT` HERE and nowhere else — a variable REPLACES a
# published port, where a `ports:` entry in the service's compose file APPENDS to
# kit's and ends up with two.
KIT_POSTGRES_PORT=15500
KIT_POSTGRES_PASSWORD=cafaye

# This service's own database, from the HOST rather than from inside the compose
# network. It is darkroom's own; there is no shared database and no cross-service
# query.
#
# THREE THINGS CHANGED HERE WHEN THIS SERVICE MOVED ONTO THE SHARED CLUSTER, and
# all three are consequences of kit's defaults rather than choices:
#
#   the port        kit publishes on 15500, not 5432. 5432 was this service's own
#                   container, and `ports:` APPENDS across two compose files, so a
#                   `ports:` block would have left postgres on 15500 AND 5432.
#   the database    `darkroom`, not `darkroom_test`. The init script creates the
#                   database NAMED by its `KIT_POSTGRES_DATABASES` entry.
#   the password    the cluster's, not `darkroom`. Every role the init script
#                   creates takes `POSTGRES_PASSWORD`, so a per-service literal
#                   here is a credential no role holds.
#
# Keep this in step with `DATABASE_URL` in `docker-compose.yml`, which is the same
# pair with `postgres` in place of `localhost`.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://darkroom:cafaye@localhost:15500/darkroom?sslmode=disable

# The URL the APPLICATION logs in with, and it is `darkroom_app`.
#
# `10-cluster.sh` provisions TWO roles from one `KIT_POSTGRES_DATABASES` entry:
# `darkroom` owns the database and runs migrations, `darkroom_app` owns nothing and
# is granted DML per table. The URL above stays `darkroom` because it is the
# migration and deploy credential, and a service that migrates its own schema cannot
# stop needing the owner. This one is what a request path uses.
#
# It is not a hardening nicety. `assets` and `asset_variants` carry FORCE ROW LEVEL
# SECURITY, so the owner is subject to their policies like everyone else — and the
# login role additionally CANNOT `ALTER TABLE ... DISABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` or
# `DROP POLICY`. A leaked connection string for this role is a read-only one.
#
# `TEST_DATABASE_URL` below is deliberately NOT this role: the test harness creates
# schemas, which needs the owner.
DARKROOM_DATABASE_URL=postgres://darkroom_app:cafaye@localhost:15500/darkroom?sslmode=disable

# identity's JWKS. The keys are cached by kid with a bounded TTL and refreshed
# on an unknown kid, so identity is never on the hot path.
DARKROOM_JWKS_URL=https://identity.cafaye.com/.well-known/jwks.json
DARKROOM_ISSUER=https://identity.cafaye.com
DARKROOM_AUDIENCE=darkroom

# --- the event bus ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# OPTIONAL, AND THE ONLY ADDRESS IN THIS FILE THAT IS. With it unset no
# publisher is built and no loop starts: rows accumulate in `outbox_events` and
# drain the moment a broker appears, which is the whole of the argument for
# publishing from a table rather than from the request path. See README, "Events
# and the outbox".
#
# It is the STACK'S name and not this service's, deliberately. NATS_URL is what
# kit's compose declares the broker under and what identity reads, so two
# services on one bus is one variable. A `DARKROOM_NATS_URL` would be a second
# copy of the address to keep in step with the first.
#
# WITH IT SET THE STACK MUST ALSO DECLARE A STREAM COVERING `darkroom.>`. A
# JetStream publish to a subject no stream matches is refused, the row stays
# unpublished with `attempts` climbing, and no event ever leaves the table. That
# is the loud direction, and it is visible with:
#
#   psql "$DATABASE_URL" -Atc "select count(*) from outbox_events where published_at is null"
#
# NATS_URL=nats://localhost:4222

# --- server ---------------------------------------------------------------

PORT=8080
DARKROOM_BIND_ADDR=0.0.0.0
# NOT READ, and it is here because deleting it would look like a decision to
# remove a tuning knob. `darkroomdb.Open` owns the pool size and sets `MaxConns`
# from the shared cluster's per-role cap (`ALTER ROLE ... CONNECTION LIMIT`), so
# a variable that looked like it configured it would be a lie in a deployment
# manifest. "a variable this build ignores is a lie in a deployment manifest."
# DARKROOM_DB_MAX_CONNECTIONS=10

DARKROOM_LOG_LEVEL=info
# `json` in production, `human` locally. The Go default is `json` unconditionally
# — it does NOT sniff stdout for a terminal, because "which format am I" is a
# question whose answer changes between two runs of the same command.
DARKROOM_LOG_FORMAT=json

# READ, LOGGED AT STARTUP, AND IT GATES NOTHING. There is no build flag and no
# feature: `internal/auth` has exactly one constructor, `auth.New`, and it is
# always on. The value is in the file because a compose file that dropped it
# would look like a deliberate removal rather than a no-op, and the smaller
# surprise is to keep it and say what it does.
DARKROOM_ENV=development

# --- object storage -------------------------------------------------------
#
# `memory` in development: uploads are lost on restart, which is fine, and it
# is the reason a worktree needs no bucket credential to start.
DARKROOM_OBJECT_STORE=memory

# For s3 — the deployment build, and the image's default:
# DARKROOM_OBJECT_STORE=s3
# DARKROOM_S3_BUCKET=darkroom-media
# DARKROOM_S3_REGION=us-east-1
# Optional, for an S3-compatible service that is not AWS.
# DARKROOM_S3_ENDPOINT=https://minio.internal:9000
# DARKROOM_S3_PATH_STYLE=true

# For Cloudflare R2 — the same `s3` store, not a different one:
#
#   DARKROOM_S3_ENDPOINT=https://<ACCOUNT_ID>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
#
# DARKROOM_S3_REGION is OPTIONAL here and defaults to `auto`, which is R2's
# region. `us-east-1` and an empty value are accepted (R2 documents them as
# aliases) and are signed as `auto` anyway. Any other region is refused at
# startup, and so is `auto` with no endpoint — AWS has no `auto` region, and a
# bucket-only configuration would otherwise resolve s3.amazonaws.com.
#
# Path-style addressing is forced for an R2 endpoint; DARKROOM_S3_PATH_STYLE has
# no say. Credentials are an R2 API token, read by the AWS SDK's own chain:
# DARKROOM_OBJECT_STORE=s3
# DARKROOM_S3_BUCKET=darkroom-media
# DARKROOM_S3_ENDPOINT=https://<ACCOUNT_ID>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=<R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID>
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=<R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY>
#
# Nothing in darkroom asks the bucket to record a checksum: R2 has no
# FULL_OBJECT SHA-256, so the checksum in the asset row is computed by reading
# the object back at complete time. See README "The storage boundary".

# --- development only -----------------------------------------------------
#
# THERE IS NO DEV JWT SECRET IN THIS TREE, and its absence is the point.
#
# The Rust binary could return an HMAC verifier when `DARKROOM_ENV=development`
# and refused to start if the secret were set anywhere else. `internal/auth` has
# no such constructor, and adding one would be a security surface nobody asked
# for, so the dev path is GONE rather than dormant: `auth.New` is the only
# verifier and it is always on. `DARKROOM_ENV` is read, logged, and gates
# nothing.
#
# A development deployment therefore needs a real JWKS endpoint — the values
# above are it. Do not reinstate DARKROOM_DEV_JWT_SECRET expecting it to work.

# --- tests ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The database tests SKIP at runtime when no database answers, so `go test ./...`
# is green on a bare machine. A skipped test proves nothing, so run the gate,
# which passes TEST_DATABASE_URL through to the tiers that need one:
#
#   docker compose --project-directory . \
#     -f <kit>/templates/compose/docker-compose.yml \
#     -f ./docker-compose.yml up -d --wait
#   ./bin/prime --db
#
# `bin/tier-counts` then reads the counts out of that log and FAILS on any
# `--- SKIP:` in a database tier, so a tier that ran and touched nothing cannot
# pass. ./bin/prime alone is the fast loop and is NOT the gate — it reports the
# database tests as `ok` without reaching one.
#
# TEST_DATABASE_URL is the HOST-side URL, so `localhost` and
# `${KIT_POSTGRES_PORT}`, and the same cluster password as `DATABASE_URL` above.
# No `--test-threads=1`: each test applies the migrations into a schema of its
# own and truncates only that schema, so they run in parallel. They still write
# to the database named above — a schema each, but the same database — so
# pointing them at one holding data you want is how you get fifty-eight schemas
# dropped next to it. See README, "These suites truncate the tables they touch."
#
# TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://darkroom:cafaye@localhost:15500/darkroom?sslmode=disable
#
# `--db` NEEDS DARKROOM_DATABASE_URL TOO, and refuses to start without it. Both are
# above, already set and uncommented — `DARKROOM_DATABASE_URL` near the top of this
# file, `TEST_DATABASE_URL` here. Neither is commented out, so `set -a; . ./.env`
# hands the gate both and the guard passes.
#
# The second one is not a second database. `TestTheLoginRoleCanDoTheWork` is the only
# test in the repository that connects as `darkroom_app` — the LOGIN role, the one
# the RLS policies apply to — and every other test in that tier connects as `darkroom`,
# the owner, because the harness needs CREATE to make a schema at all. With only
# `TEST_DATABASE_URL` set the tier runs 14 tests of 15 and the fifteenth skips with a
# reason; `bin/tier-counts`' floor of 15 then fails on a run every test passed, which
# is worse than a red test because it looks like a gate bug. Required instead, the
# gate names what it is missing in under a second.
